From: intelxe-build <build@localhost>
Date: Fri, 13 Nov 2026 17:23:42 +0300
Subject: [PATCH 6/7] clang: pass C++ records in registers by Apple's rule on
 Darwin

Apple's clang, which builds libc++ and the other C++ libraries of the
platform (and libsycl, the UR adapters and UMF here: their CMake uses the
system compiler), decides whether a C++ record is passed in registers by
the Clang 4 rule on Darwin: a trivial copy constructor and a trivial
destructor, the move constructor not considered. This clang used the
C++11 rule, which also wants one copy or move constructor that is not
deleted. For a record whose copy and move constructors are all deleted
the two disagree: Apple's clang passes it in registers, this clang by
address.

sycl::detail::type_erased_cgfo_ty is such a record (two pointers, every
copy and move deleted) and is passed by value to libsycl's
HandlerAccess::preProcess and postProcess. A program built by this
clang passed its address in %rsi; libsycl, built by Apple's clang, read
the object pointer from %rsi and the invoker from %rdx and called
whatever %rdx held: a call through a null pointer in the first
sycl::reduction on a discrete GPU (getGroupsCounterAccDiscrete).

getCallingConvKind now gives the Clang 4 rule on Darwin targets, so this
clang and Apple's agree on every record (checked over a set of copy,
move and destructor combinations on x86_64 and arm64).
---
 clang/lib/Basic/TargetInfo.cpp | 8 +++++++-
 1 file changed, 7 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/clang/lib/Basic/TargetInfo.cpp b/clang/lib/Basic/TargetInfo.cpp
index 46b5bdecb..df8817e92 100644
--- a/clang/lib/Basic/TargetInfo.cpp
+++ b/clang/lib/Basic/TargetInfo.cpp
@@ -627,8 +627,14 @@ ParsedTargetAttr TargetInfo::parseTargetAttr(StringRef Features) const {
 
 TargetInfo::CallingConvKind
 TargetInfo::getCallingConvKind(bool ClangABICompat4) const {
+  // Darwin: Apple's clang, which builds the system's C++ libraries (libc++) and
+  // every other C++ library on the platform, passes C++ records by the Clang 4
+  // rule too: in registers when the copy constructor and the destructor are
+  // trivial, whatever the move constructor is. A record whose copy and move
+  // constructors are all deleted therefore goes in registers there, and
+  // indirectly by the C++11 rule; the two sides of a call then disagree.
   if (getCXXABI() != TargetCXXABI::Microsoft &&
-      (ClangABICompat4 || getTriple().isPS4()))
+      (ClangABICompat4 || getTriple().isPS4() || getTriple().isOSDarwin()))
     return CCK_ClangABI4OrPS4;
   return CCK_Default;
 }
